REPRODUCER (probed vs perl 5.40.3):

    sub setit { $_[0] = "via-alias" }
    tie my %t, 'TH'; $t{k} = "orig"; setit($t{k}); print "1 $t{k}\n";
    setit($t{j} = "orig"); print "2 $t{j}\n";
    package TH; sub TIEHASH { bless {}, shift } sub STORE { $_[0]{$_[1]} = $_[2] } sub FETCH { $_[0]{$_[1]} }

perl: `1 via-alias` / `2 via-alias`.   PCL (s536d tree): `1 via-alias` / `2 orig`.

WHY (read, not fixed): s536d's #4622 generates an element assignment in a user-sub argument slot in lvalue context, so the emission is `(pl-setit (p-setf (p-gethash-box %t "j") "orig"))`; for an untied hash p-setf of the box place answers that box, for a tied one (p-gethash-box -> %p-tie-elem-box) the value p-setf answers is not the tie element box (the plain element argument `setit($t{k})` passes `(p-gethash-argbox %t "k")` = the tie element box and aliases).  Same family as #4303's `($t{k} = V) =~ s///` on a tied hash -- probe that too.
WHERE TO START: cl/pcl-runtime.lisp `p-setf`'s arm for a `p-gethash-box` / `p-aref-box` place over a tied container; the store should answer the tie element box it stored through.


